verdict.corrected:
Suggested text for the replication write-up:

"Across both tests (mdplan and mdrep, each as drawn, with the information changed, and in each writer's wording alone: 500 question-runs), v10 never scored below v9. It was higher on 14 runs, which are 12 distinct question-and-wording cases plus one changed-information copy.

The bank's own lessons were unsure of the kind of answer on only 4 writer templates:
- writer 1's linked-ticket status;
- writer 1's PR-ticket due date;
- writer 1's PR-ticket status;
- writer 3's PR-ticket due date ('supposed to be done by').

The form changed the bank's plan only on 2 of them, and so on 2 wordings: writer 1's linked-ticket status and writer 3's 'supposed to be done by'. There it turned 14 wrong answers right. On one other question (first test, ENG-4823) it changed one wrong answer to another.

The form's field is also applied wherever its kind agrees with lessons that are sure (460 of the 500 runs), but it never changed a plan there.

So the evidence that the form helps rests on 2 wordings. 'No harm' holds on these two sets but not in general: on an earlier set (the retest, mdfresh) the form lost one question, a PR-ticket assignee answered with a key (0.984 against 0.968)."
